arXiv:2510.03553v1 [cs.CL] 03 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">CCD-Bench: Probing Cultural Conflict in Large Language Model Decision-Making</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hasibur Rahman
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hanan Salam
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Although Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly implicated in interpersonal and societal decision-making, their ability to navigate explicit conflicts between legitimately different cultural value systems remains largely unexamined. Existing benchmarks predominantly target cultural knowledge (CulturalBench), value prediction (WorldValuesBench), or single-axis bias diagnostics (CDEval); none, however, evaluate how LLMs adjudicate when multiple culturally grounded values directly clash. We address this gap with CCD-Bench, a benchmark that assesses LLM decision-making under overt cross-cultural value conflict. CCD-Bench comprises 2,182 open-ended dilemmas spanning seven domains, each paired with exactly ten anonymized response options corresponding to the ten GLOBE cultural clusters, which represent the organizational behavior of 62 societies. These dilemmas are presented using a Stratified Latin Square to mitigate ordering effects. We evaluate 17 leading non-reasoning LLMs. LLMs disproportionately prefer Nordic Europe (mean 20.2%) and Germanic Europe (12.4%), while the options for Eastern Europe and the Middle East &amp; North Africa are underrepresented (5.6–5.8%). Although 87.9% of rationales reference two or more GLOBE dimensions, this apparent pluralism is largely superficial: LLMs repeatedly recombine a narrow subset of Future Orientation and Performance Orientation, and rarely ground choices in Assertiveness or Gender Egalitarianism (both <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;3\%</annotation></semantics></math>). Ordering effects are negligible (Cramér’s <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V&lt;0.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V&lt;0.10</annotation></semantics></math>), and symmetrized KL divergence indicates LLMs clustering by developer lineage rather than geography. Taken together, these patterns suggest that contemporary alignment pipelines encourage a consensus-oriented, progress-centric worldview that underserves scenarios demanding explicit power negotiation, rights-based reasoning, or gender-aware analysis. CCD-Bench thus shifts evaluation from isolated bias detection to pluralistic decision making, revealing that current LLMs maintain Western-centric, consensus-oriented preferences even when confronted with ten equally valid, culturally diverse alternatives, and underscoring the need for alignment strategies that substantively engage with diverse worldviews.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">New York University Abu Dhabi, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">hasibur.rahman@nyu.edu, hanan.salam@nyu.edu</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language models (LLMs) have been actively explored as decision mediators in interpersonal and social contexts, including conflict resolution and moral judgment tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pires et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koçak, Puranam, and Yeğin 2024</a>)</cite>. Given that LLMs have become ubiquitous in everyday applications through conversational user interfaces, millions of people worldwide are engaging with LLMs in socially mediated roles. A seemingly mundane query, “How should I email my boss to request leave?”, demands not only factual accuracy but culturally astute counsel that respects prevailing norms and values.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the widespread adoption of LLMs exposes a pressing challenge: can LLMs align their recommendations with the full spectrum of global cultural values? A growing body of evidence shows that LLMs inherit salient cultural leanings from their training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karinshak et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu, Song, and Zhang 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AlKhamissi et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Users, therefore, risk receiving responses that, although coherent, may be culturally narrow or inconsistent. Consider the question “How do you perceive the sharing of responsibilities within the family?” a query that elicits fundamentally different yet equally legitimate responses across cultures, from merit-based distribution to age-based hierarchies to gender egalitarianism. While prompt-engineering strategies that foreground contextual cues offer partial relief <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong, Yun, and Sun 2024</a>)</cite>, a deeper dilemma emerges:
<span id="Sx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”How do LLMs adjudicate situations where legitimate cultural values, such as hierarchy vs egalitarianism, or group harmony vs personal initiative, directly collide?”</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AlKhamissi et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we adopt “cultural trends” rather than “biases” to describe LLM inclinations, as these patterns may not imply harm but reveal limitations in serving pluralistic contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Leading benchmarks, such as MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. 2021</a>)</cite> and ELO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, prioritize factual accuracy and stylistic quality, thereby overlooking the cultural nuance and social intelligence required in real-world interactions. Early alignment paradigms foreground universal principles such as helpfulness, honesty, harmlessness (the “HHH” criteria) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Askell et al. 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, yet sidestep the pluralistic, culturally contingent nature of human values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barbara Herrnstein Smith 1991</a>)</cite>. Real decisions often hinge on reconciling legitimate but conflicting value systems, a challenge for which the “HHH” framework offers little guidance. In contrast to existing benchmarks<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. 2024</a>)</cite> that simply record where a model sits on individual cultural axes, a current limitation in the field is that no prior benchmark explores such explicit cultural conflicts, where legitimate values directly clash, leaving a gap in understanding LLMs’ decision-making in pluralistic scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we introduce <span id="Sx1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CCD-Bench</span>, the <span id="Sx1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Culture-Conflict Decision Benchmark</span>. CCD-Bench probes how LLMs navigate value-plural dilemmas in which multiple worldviews are equally defensible. Our diagnostic suite evaluates the 17 most-used LLMs (excluding specialized reasoning models), embedding all culturally salient information directly in the prompt to isolate the LLMs’ intrinsic decision tendencies. CCD-Bench constitutes a paradigm shift in LLMs’ cultural evaluation. The benchmark comprises 2,182 dilemmas across seven domains: Arts, Education, Family, Wellness, Work, Scientific, and Lifestyle, adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Building on the insight that many LLMs already encode cultural frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karinshak et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, we generate ten anonymized candidate answers for each dilemma via a state-of-the-art reasoning model, followed by human verification. Each answer embodies one of GLOBE’s cultural clusters: Anglo, Latin Europe, Nordic Europe, Germanic Europe, Eastern Europe, Latin America, Sub-Saharan Africa, Middle East and North Africa, Southern Asia, and Confucian Asia, representing 62 societies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">House et al. 2004</a>)</cite>. Rather than locating an LLM on isolated cultural dimensions, we observe how it behaves when confronted with competing imperatives. CCD-Bench embeds the cultural trade-off within the dilemma itself, offers ten culturally-diverse responses aligned with GLOBE cultural framework, and asks the LLM to select and justify one, thereby exposing its cultural reasoning processes.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We advance three contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce CCD-Bench, the first benchmark for evaluating LLMs on navigating complex cultural value conflicts, comprising 2,182 dilemmas across seven domains with ten culturally distinct, anonymized responses per dilemma, drawn from GLOBE clusters.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a robust pipeline for generating and verifying culturally authentic alternatives, utilizing multi-cluster prompting, similarity pruning, human annotation, and a Stratified Latin Square presentation to facilitate a fair assessment of value pluralism.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate 17 leading LLMs on CCD-Bench, revealing systematic patterns in how different models navigate cultural conflicts and the extent to which they default to particular cultural frameworks.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Work</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compelling evidence already shows that LLMs carry distinct cultural fingerprints. Prompting GPT-4 in English, for instance, yields analytic, independence-laden reasoning, whereas Chinese prompts evoke holistic, interdependent values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu, Song, and Zhang 2025</a>)</cite>. Persona prompting can accentuate these leanings, but cannot erase them. Likewise, <span id="Sx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CDEval</span> finds GPT-4 skewed toward low power distance and high individualism, while Baichuan-13B and Qwen-7B exhibit stronger collectivist tendencies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Yet, its binary, one-dimension-at-a-time format flattens pluralism. <span id="Sx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-GLOBE</span> moves to open-ended prompts, uncovering tonal and rhetorical differences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karinshak et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, but confines itself to bilateral comparisons and still avoids explicit value collisions. A cross-cultural survey alignment study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AlKhamissi et al. 2024</a>)</cite> demonstrates improvements through “Anthropological Prompting”, but falls short of testing what happens when legitimate values directly clash.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Other benchmarks further highlight cultural gaps in LLMs but similarly stop short of probing value conflicts. For instance, <span id="Sx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CulturalBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiu et al. 2024</a>)</cite> features 1,227 human-written questions across 45 regions and 17 topics to assess cultural knowledge, with variants for different difficulty levels. However, it focuses on recall and ambiguity handling rather than decision-making amid conflicting norms. <span id="Sx2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">WorldValuesBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. 2024</a>)</cite> draws from the World Values Survey to create millions of examples for predicting demographic-conditioned values, evaluating alignment via distribution metrics, but emphasizes prediction over resolution of conflicting perspectives. <span id="Sx2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BLEnD</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Myung et al. 2024</a>)</cite> tests everyday cultural knowledge with 52,600 QA pairs across 16 countries and 13 languages, revealing performance disparities for low-resource cultures, although it targets factual recall rather than value trade-offs. CAMeL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naous et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and its extensions detect biases in associations, particularly between Arab and Western entities, using fill-in-the-blank prompts and parallel data, but center on implicit biases instead of explicit dilemmas requiring justification.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these contributions, a key limitation persists: no existing work systematically explores how LLMs adjudicate cultural value conflicts, where multiple legitimate worldviews collide in decision scenarios. This gap motivates CCD-Bench’s focus on pluralistic dilemmas with cultural conflicts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Methodology</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section describes the methodology used to construct CCD-Bench. The benchmark itself consists of 2,182 cultural dilemmas with 10 culturally-aligned response alternatives each, while this methodology details the systematic process for generating, verifying, and structuring these materials.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Generation Module</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build upon CDEval’s question corpus as it represents the most comprehensive collection of culturally-relevant scenarios. These questions were specifically designed to elicit culture-dependent responses and have been empirically shown to produce divergent answers across different cultural contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. By leveraging this validated foundation rather than generating questions de novo, we ensure our benchmark captures genuine cultural decision points while maintaining comparability with existing cultural AI evaluation efforts.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with the 2,953 short–form cultural questions curated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. From these, we select 2204 open-ended questions that can plausibly elicit divergent advice when viewed through different cultural lenses by removing questions that required binary options.
Each scenario is categorized into one of seven application domains: Arts, Education, Family, Wellness, Work, Scientific, and Lifestyle.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For every dilemma <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate a set of <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">culturally distinctive</span> answers <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{c}\}_{c=1}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{c}\}_{c=1}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>, one per GLOBE cluster <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{</annotation></semantics></math>Anglo, Latin Europe, …, Confucian Asia<math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">House et al. 2004</a>)</cite>.
A single call to a state-of-the-art reasoning model (OpenAI <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o3-2025-04-16</span>) produces these alternatives in parallel by embedding fenced <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;cultural identity&gt;</span> directives directly in the prompt. The <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;cultural identity&gt;</span> contained the anonymized GLOBE results of each cultural cluster. A detailed prompt structure can be found in the public release. The reasoning model must return: (i) a <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">one–sentence answer</span> that omits explicit self-reference to culture and (ii) a <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">one–sentence rationale</span> that references GLOBE cultural values.
Based on the findings from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balepur, Rudinger, and Boyd-Graber (2025)</a></cite>, we ask the LLM to provide the rationale to increase coherence with the specific <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;cultural identity&gt;</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Similarity pruning and regeneration.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our prompting should theoretically yield diverse responses based on <span id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;cultural identity&gt;</span> directives, redundancy emerged at the lexical level when generated responses of different cultures express similar pragmatic advice using overlapping vocabulary. As redundant answers reduce discriminative power, we therefore compute pairwise <span id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MinHash Jaccard</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cann et al. 2025</a>)</cite> similarity over 3-gram shingles for each 10-tuple. Any pair with similarity <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\!&gt;\!0.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&gt;</mo><mn>0.70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\!&gt;\!0.70</annotation></semantics></math> triggers a regeneration of the higher-similarity answer with a maximum of two attempts. We selected <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.70</annotation></semantics></math> based on preliminary analysis showing that answer pairs above this threshold exhibited minimal semantic distinction when manually reviewed, while pairs below 0.70 maintained meaningful cultural perspectives.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Due to not meeting the threshold among 2,204 questions, 22 questions were removed. Across the final 2,182 dilemmas, the pair-wise <span id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MinHash Jaccard</span> similarity between the ten alternatives has a mean of <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.123" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.123</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.123</annotation></semantics></math> with a standard deviation of <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.109" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.109</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.109</annotation></semantics></math> (N = <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{10}{2}\times 2182" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2182</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{10}{2}\times 2182</annotation></semantics></math> comparisons), confirming that regeneration successfully suppresses near-duplicate content while preserving lexical variety. Our similarity pruning specifically targets lexical redundancy while preserving semantic and cultural distinctiveness, as validated by our human verification step.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Option ordering.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pezeshkpour and Hruschka (2024)</a></cite> demonstrated that the order of options in multiple-choice questions (MCQs) significantly impacts the performance of Large Language Models (LLMs). To neutralize serial-position effects, we employ a <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="Sx3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stratified Latin Square</span>, a domain-specific adaptation of the standard Latin square design, where independent Latin squares are generated and applied separately for each individual domain to ensure balanced option ordering within those subgroups, rather than globally across the entire corpus. Each cultural alternative appears exactly once in every ordinal slot (1–10) and precedes/follows every other alternative exactly once across the whole corpus, satisfying both row and column orthogonality. A detailed evaluation prompt structure can be found in the public release.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Verification Module</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A human annotator reviews every candidate set. The annotator confirms: (i) each generated option would be viewed as <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasonable</span> within the target cluster, (ii) does not mention the cultural cluster directly, and (iii) is semantically distinct from the other nine options. Non-conforming options are regenerated until all criteria are met, with a maximum of two attempts. None of the earlier 2,182 dilemmas were removed at this step.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Evaluation Module</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analytic pipeline decomposes evaluation logs into three layers: descriptive statistics, null-hypothesis significance tests, and robust similarity/diversity indices.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Descriptive layer.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each evaluation file, we record (i) absolute frequency <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> of each cultural cluster <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\{1,\dots,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\{1,\dots,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>, (ii) display position distribution <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=(\pi_{1},\dots,\pi_{10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=(\pi_{1},\dots,\pi_{10})</annotation></semantics></math>, and (iii) domain-specific cluster counts. Raw tallies are used without smoothing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Position-bias test.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under null hypothesis <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of equal likelihood for <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10</annotation></semantics></math> positions, let <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{O}</annotation></semantics></math> be observed counts and <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}=(N/k,\dots,N/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}=(N/k,\dots,N/k)</annotation></semantics></math> expected counts, with <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\sum_{i}O_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\sum_{i}O_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> goodness-of-fit test yields</p>
<table id="Sx3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{(O_{i}-E_{i})^{2}}{E_{i}},\qquad V=\sqrt{\frac{\chi^{2}}{N(k-1)}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{(O_{i}-E_{i})^{2}}{E_{i}},\qquad V=\sqrt{\frac{\chi^{2}}{N(k-1)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where Cramér’s <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> follows thresholds: negligible <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.10</annotation></semantics></math>, small <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.30</annotation></semantics></math>, medium <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.50</annotation></semantics></math>, large <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.50</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harald Cramér 1999</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Domain–cluster association.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times C</annotation></semantics></math> contingency table <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}=[T_{ij}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}=[T_{ij}]</annotation></semantics></math> for domains (<math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>) vs. clusters (<math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>). Pearson’s <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}(\mathbf{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(\mathbf{T})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D-1)(C-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D-1)(C-1)</annotation></semantics></math> degrees of freedom is reported with standardized residuals <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ij}=\frac{T_{ij}-E_{ij}}{\sqrt{E_{ij}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ij}=\frac{T_{ij}-E_{ij}}{\sqrt{E_{ij}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to identify over/under-represented pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agresti 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Latin-square balance.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Option orderings use a <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> Latin square <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley 1958</a>)</cite>. Let <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> matrix of cluster appearances per position across <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> questions. Imbalance is measured as</p>
<table id="Sx3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\bigl\|A-(Q/10)\mathbf{1}\bigr\|_{1},\qquad\mathrm{BS}=100\left(1-\frac{\Delta}{1.8\cdot 10Q}\right)\%," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>BS</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1.8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\bigl\|A-(Q/10)\mathbf{1}\bigr\|_{1},\qquad\mathrm{BS}=100\left(1-\frac{\Delta}{1.8\cdot 10Q}\right)\%,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with 1.8 as the worst-case normalization factor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diversity metrics.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For cluster frequencies <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and sorted counts <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{(1)}\leq\dots\leq x_{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{(1)}\leq\dots\leq x_{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\mathbf{p})=-\sum_{c}p_{c}\log_{2}p_{c}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>c</mi></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathbf{p})=-\sum_{c}p_{c}\log_{2}p_{c},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="Sx3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{n}\Bigl(n+1-\frac{2}{\sum_{i}x_{(i)}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(n+1-i)x_{(i)}\Bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{n}\Bigl(n+1-\frac{2}{\sum_{i}x_{(i)}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(n+1-i)x_{(i)}\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shannon and Weaver 1949</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dorfman 1979</a>)</cite>.
For each statistic <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we draw <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=5,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=5,000</annotation></semantics></math> bootstrap replicates and report [2.5, 97.5] percentiles as 95% confidence bands <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Efron and Tibshirani 1998</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03553v1/imgs/per_model_cluster_radar.png" id="Sx3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:1075/873;" width="1075" height="873" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Cultural Cluster Selection Frequencies</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model similarity.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For LLMs with distributions <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, symmetrized Kullback–Leibler divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kullback 1997</a>)</cite> is</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{sym}}(P,Q)=\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{c}\left[P_{c}\log\frac{P_{c}}{Q_{c}}+Q_{c}\log\frac{Q_{c}}{P_{c}}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>sym</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>c</mi></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{sym}}(P,Q)=\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{c}\left[P_{c}\log\frac{P_{c}}{Q_{c}}+Q_{c}\log\frac{Q_{c}}{P_{c}}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">adding <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-10}</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The matrix feeds average-linkage hierarchical clustering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sneath and Sokal 1973</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cluster–position bias.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Repeating position-bias per cluster gives Cramér’s <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>; mean <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{V}=\frac{1}{10}\sum_{c}V_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>c</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{V}=\frac{1}{10}\sum_{c}V_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the model-bias score.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pluralism in rationales.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if rationale <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> cites <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> GLOBE dimensions (<math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise), for <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>. The pluralism rate is <math id="Sx3.SSx3.SSS0.Px8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{plural}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i}I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>plural</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{plural}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i}I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, reported per LLM and ensemble to assess value-reasoning breadth.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experiments</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we utilize the 17 most commonly used LLMs for non-technical tasks from OpenRouter. Our inquiry is structured around five questions:</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">When offered an array of culturally valid options, do LLMs still prefer certain clusters or values?(RQ1)</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A pronounced Nordic trend.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every single model in our sample selects the <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nordic Europe</span> option most often. The share ranges from <math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> (Cohere <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">command-r</span>) to a remarkable <math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> (Anthropic <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude-4 Sonnet</span>), with an across-model mean of <math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=20.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>20.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=20.2\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Put differently, the median model chooses Nordic-style advice roughly <math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> more often than chance, signaling an entrenched preference for highly egalitarian, consensus-driven solutions. Figure <a href="#Sx3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Diversity metrics. ‣ Evaluation Module ‣ Methodology ‣ CCD-Bench: Probing Cultural Conflict in Large Language Model Decision-Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> visualizes the raw selection frequencies for all <math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math> LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Secondary leanings.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two clusters emerge as consistent runners-up: <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Germanic Europe</span> (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=12.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>12.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=12.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>): 7 LLMs list it as their second-most frequent choice. And <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sub-Saharan Africa</span> (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=11.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>11.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=11.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>): likewise second for seven LLMs, underscoring that collectivist but low-power-distance reasoning resonates with many systems. A smaller subset (three LLMs, all OpenAI or Google) places the <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anglo</span> cluster in second position, yet its average share (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>) still lags behind the Nordic and Germanic means.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Under-represented clusters.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the lower end of the spectrum lie <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Eastern Europe</span> (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and the <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Middle East &amp; North Africa</span> (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.8\%</annotation></semantics></math>). No LLM exceeds an <math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> selection rate for either group, and the minimum for Eastern Europe drops to just <math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> in Anthropic <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude-4 Sonnet</span>. These figures suggest that narratives foregrounding high power distance or strong in-group collectivism are comparatively neglected.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Between-model variability.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cluster preferences vary in magnitude, but not in direction. The coefficient of variation is smallest for <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latin Europe</span> (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CV}=0.09" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CV</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>0.09</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CV}=0.09</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that LLMs converge on a modest but stable share for this cluster. In contrast, <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anglo</span> selections display the greatest relative spread (<math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CV}=0.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CV</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>0.23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CV}=0.23</annotation></semantics></math>), hinting that “mainstream Western” advice is more LLM-specific than often assumed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Which values do the LLMs foreground?</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Which values do the LLMs foreground? ‣ When offered an array of culturally valid options, do LLMs still prefer certain clusters or values?(RQ1) ‣ Experiments ‣ CCD-Bench: Probing Cultural Conflict in Large Language Model Decision-Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows how often each system cites the nine GLOBE dimensions when justifying its selections. Two broad patterns emerge.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03553v1/imgs/dimension_comparison.png" id="Sx4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/475;" width="698" height="475" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>GLOBE Dimension Mentioned by LLMs</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="Sx4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A future-oriented, high-achievement consensus.</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Across the 17 LLMs, four dimensions dominate the rationales:</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx4.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Dimension</th>
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Mean Mention (%)</th>
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">s.d.</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">In-Group Collectivism</th>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">38.2</td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">9.5</td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Performance Orientation</th>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">37.1</td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">9.4</td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Future Orientation</th>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">35.0</td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">7.4</td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Institutional Collectivism</th>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">33.5</td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">10.5</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Most frequently mentioned cultural dimensions across 17 LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.I2.i1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All four sit well above the chance baseline of 11% (one-ninth), indicating that, irrespective of the cultural cluster they ultimately pick, LLMs systematically justify their advice in terms of group solidarity, long-range thinking, and getting things done.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low appetite for confrontation or gender talk.</span>
At the opposite extreme, two dimensions are almost absent:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.I2.i2.1" class="ltx_logical-block">
                
<div id="Sx4.I2.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Assertiveness: <math id="Sx4.I2.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Sx4.I2.i2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma=2.6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma=2.6)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<p id="Sx4.I2.i2.p2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Gender Egalitarianism: <math id="Sx4.I2.i2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Sx4.I2.i2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma=2.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma=2.5)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
              
</div>
<div id="Sx4.I2.i2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">No LLM mentions assertiveness in more than 11% of its rationales, and the highest rate for gender egalitarianism is 13.6% (Anthropic <span id="Sx4.I2.i2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude-4 Sonnet</span>). Even LLMs that frequently invoke <span id="Sx4.I2.i2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Power Distance</span> (e.g. <span id="Sx4.I2.i2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude-3 Sonnet</span>, 48.6%) do so via “respect for hierarchy” rather than through a direct appeal to forceful negotiation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Between-model contrasts.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the aggregate rankings are stable, individual profiles diverge:
(i)<span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anthropic LLMs</span> couple very high <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Performance Orientation</span> <math id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> with the steepest <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Power Distance</span> scores, mirroring their Nordic cluster preference for meritocratic—but-still-hierarchical solutions. (ii) <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Google’s Gemini 2.x</span> variants under-index on <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Institutional Collectivism</span> (13.6–31.6%) yet over-index on <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Humane Orientation</span>, suggesting a more person-centred rationale style. (iii) <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenAI’s GPT-4.1</span> is an outlier for <span id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Group Collectivism</span> (54.7%), framing advice in terms of familial or team loyalty more than any other system.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Convergent evidence across clusters and evaluative dimensions yields a single, coherent conclusion. When confronted with ten anonymized, order-balanced alternatives, each of the 17 LLMs shows a marked preference for the normative frameworks of Northern and Western Europe, especially the Nordic and Germanic clusters, signaling an alignment imprint rather than stochastic variation. Their accompanying rationales tell the same story: although explicitly invited to invoke any of GLOBE’s nine cultural dimensions, LLMs repeatedly draw on a narrow, ostensibly uncontentious moral repertoire, future orientation, collective responsibility, and performance achievement, while rarely appealing to assertiveness or gender egalitarianism. Taken together, the results suggest prevailing alignment pipelines embed a conciliatory, progress-centred worldview emblematic of affluent liberal democracies—functional in many settings yet inadequate to capture humanity’s full cultural breadth.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Are these preferences domain-specific (e.g., religion vs. business) or globally consistent? (RQ2)</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To probe whether the cultural leanings uncovered in RQ1 persist across topical contexts, we split the 2,182 dilemmas into the seven CCD-Bench domains.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dominant Nordic trend.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">every</span> domain, the <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nordic Europe</span> option remains the single most frequent choice. Its mean share varies from a high of 23.9% in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Family</span> to a low of 15.6% in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Lifestyle</span>, but the ranking never drops below first place. Hence, the headline bias identified in RQ1 is not an artefact of a particular topic, such as business or science; it is a cross-domain constant of the evaluated LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain:</span> Sci., Work, Educ., Wellness, Lifestyle, Family, Arts</td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nordic share (%):</span> 21.9, 20.6, 21.2, 21.0, 15.6, 23.9, 15.8</td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rank</span> 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Which clusters fluctuate?</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the top slot remains stable, the lower tail shifts according to context. The <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">least</span> referenced cluster varies by domain: <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eastern Europe</span> in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Science</span>, <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Middle East &amp; North Africa</span> in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Work</span> and <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Education</span>, <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latin America</span> in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Wellness</span>, <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latin Europe</span> in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Lifestyle</span>, and <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Confucian Asia</span> in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Arts</span>. Domain content thus nudges <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which</span> minority perspective is discounted. <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Germanic Europe</span> and <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sub-Saharan Africa</span> exhibit the widest domain variance (s.d. <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> 3 pp), rising in <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Work</span> (Germanic) or <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Wellness</span> (Sub-Saharan), yet receding elsewhere.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Statistical test.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Collapsing all LLMs into a single <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> contingency matrix and applying Pearson’s <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> test yields <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=256.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>256.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=256.7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="df=54" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>54</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">df=54</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the effect size is modest: Cramér’s <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=.12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=.12</annotation></semantics></math> (95% CI [.10, .14]), labeled “small.” In other words, the domain does influence cluster selection, but the global Nordic skew significantly outweighs its magnitude.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Cultural preferences in current LLMs are <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">globally consistent</span>. Topical context shifts the edges of the distribution, deciding which clusters are least represented. Still, it does not perturb the overarching hierarchy: Nordic-style, low-power-distance, achievement-oriented advice dominates whether the user asks about science policy, workplace etiquette, family dilemmas, or the arts. The trends diagnosed in RQ1 are therefore systemic, not situational, and should be addressed at the alignment or data-curation level rather than via domain-specific patching.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Do LLMs integrate multiple cultural dimensions or default to a single axis? (RQ3)</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">High pluralism.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across the whole corpus (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=37,092" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>37,092</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=37,092</annotation></semantics></math> rationales), 87.9% cite two or more dimensions, indicating that most LLMs <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">can</span> articulate multi-faceted justifications when prompted. Yet, the model spread is broad, ranging from a low of 68.6% (Meta-Llama 4 Maverick) to a peak of 98.6% (Anthropic Claude-4 Sonnet). 7 of the 17 systems exceed the 90% mark, whereas three systems remain below 80%. High pluralism is therefore common.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pluralism <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\,{\neq}\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≠</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\,{\neq}\,</annotation></semantics></math> cultural breadth.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One might expect an LLM that distributes its selections broadly (i.e., exhibits high entropy) also to draw on more dimensions, but the data suggest otherwise. The Pearson correlation between entropy and pluralism is mildly <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">negative</span> (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=-0.22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.22</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=-0.22</annotation></semantics></math>). Cohere <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">command-r</span> exhibits the <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">highest</span> entropy (3.28) yet one of the lower pluralism scores (78.5%), while Anthropic Claude-4 shows the reverse. Integrating multiple dimensions and sampling widely across clusters are therefore distinct.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Which pairings dominate?</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two results stand out: first, a single pairing explains one-fifth of all plural rationales, with <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Future Orientation (FO) + Performance Orientation (PO)</span> appearing 6,732 times (20.6% of all plural explanations), no other pairing reaches 16%, meaning LLMs overwhelmingly justify “multi-dimensional” decisions as “long-term planning to achieve goals”; second, collectivist modifiers dominate the next tier, splicing <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Institutional Collectivism (IC)</span>, <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Group Collectivism (IGC)</span>, or <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Humane Orientation (HO)</span> onto either PO or FO, with the top six pairings, FO + PO (20.6%), HO + IGC (15.3%), IC + PO (14.4%), FO + IC (12.9%), IGC + Power Distance (PD) (12.4%), and HO + IC (10.7%), accounting for 86% of all plural rationales.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dimension-level centrality.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Summing across all pairings shows the frequency with which each GLOBE dimension enters multi-dimensional rationales: PO (16.2%), FO (15.1%), IC (14.9%), IGC (13.5%), HO (13.0%), PD (12.5%), Uncertainty Avoidance (UA) (9.5%), Gender Egalitarianism (GE) (2.7%), and Assertiveness (AS) (2.6%). The first six account for over 85% of mentions, with AS and GE remaining fringe (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>3% each).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model signatures.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The expanded per-model breakdown shows that: (i) <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anthropic models</span> over-index on the “achievement + hierarchy” tripod: PO, IC, and PD.
(ii) <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Google Gemini 2.x</span> variants distinguish themselves through frequent IGC + PO pairings, signaling a “team success” rhetoric. (iii) <span id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Meta-Llama 4 Maverick</span> is the only model where FO + PO accounts for less than 24% of its plural rationales, yet it also exhibits the lowest pluralism rate overall.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs integrate multiple cultural dimensions, but combinations are imbalanced: four mainstream values, FO, PO, and the two forms of Collectivism, dominate <math id="Sx4.SSx3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 90\%</annotation></semantics></math> of multi-dimensional rationales, while AS and GE remain marginal. Thus, models exhibit limited pluralism, relying on a narrow subset of harmonious dimensions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Do Models Exhibit Systematic Ordering Bias When Selecting Among Options? (RQ4)</h3>

<figure id="Sx4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03553v1/imgs/cluster_position_bias_analysis.png" id="Sx4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/399;" width="698" height="399" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Cultural cluster selection percentages by display position (P1–P10).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03553v1/imgs/distance_matrices.png" id="Sx4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:2091/1041;" width="2091" height="1041" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Heatmap of symmetrized KL-divergence between models’ cultural cluster selections (left), with average-linkage hierarchical clustering dendrogram (right).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our generated corpus has a Latin-square balance score of <math id="Sx4.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 99.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>99.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 99.8\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Despite employing the Stratified Latin Square design to neutralize serial and residual ordering biases, these biases may persist if models exhibit systematic preferences for certain positions (e.g., favoring early or late options). In this section, we analyze the distribution of selections across the 10 positions for each cultural cluster, aggregated over all 17 models and 2,182 dilemmas (total valid selections: 36,977; expected per position under uniformity: 3,697.7). We quantify positional bias using Cramér’s <math id="Sx4.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> per cluster, interpreting values below 0.10 as negligible. Figure <a href="#Sx4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Do Models Exhibit Systematic Ordering Bias When Selecting Among Options? (RQ4) ‣ Experiments ‣ CCD-Bench: Probing Cultural Conflict in Large Language Model Decision-Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> visualizes the percentage of selections for each cluster-position pair.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overall Positional Bias Levels.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All 10 clusters exhibit negligible positional bias, with Cramér’s <math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> values ranging from 0.0510 (Nordic Europe) to 0.0877 (Eastern Europe), confirming no small, medium, or large effects across the board. This low bias underscores the effectiveness of our Latin Square design in mitigating ordering artifacts. A detailed breakdown can be found in the public release.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mild Primacy Effect in Positions.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A modest yet discernible primacy tendency surfaces: Position 1 (P1) is chosen disproportionately in nine of the ten cultural clusters, 15.6% for Confucian Asia and 15.5% for Eastern Europe, for example, producing an aggregate deviation of 42.2 from the uniform baseline. Nordic Europe constitutes the sole exception, exhibiting a slight preference for P4 (12.7%) over P1 (11.4%). Choices in the later positions (P6–P10) remain evenly distributed, whereas P5 proves most balanced overall, with a deviation of only 8.6.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cluster-Specific Variations.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Clusters that are generally under-selected, Eastern Europe (2,086 total selections) and the Middle East (2,153), manifest the most significant relative positional biases (<math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=0.0877" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0877</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=0.0877</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=0.0797" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0797</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=0.0797</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively). Both clusters show elevated selection rates in early slots (Eastern Europe’s P1 at 15.5%) and marked declines in later ones (P8 at 6.4%). Conversely, highly favored clusters, such as Nordic Europe, register the lowest positional variance (2.34), indicating a more stable distribution across positions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these patterns confirm that the experimental protocol largely suppresses ordering effects; any residual positional bias is insufficient to compromise the cultural preference findings reported in the preceding RQs. Nonetheless, the persistent over-selection of P1 suggests that future benchmarks could benefit from enhanced randomization schemes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Do LLMs Cluster by Cultural Behavior, and Does This Pattern Correlate with Their Geographic Provenance? (RQ5)</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess similarities in cultural selection patterns and their relation to developers’ locations, we compute symmetrized Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between each pair of models’ cluster distributions, yielding a 17<math id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>17 distance matrix fed into average-linkage hierarchical clustering. Developer geographies are from public sources: US (Anthropic, Google, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI, Perplexity, xAI), China (DeepSeek, Qwen), Canada (Cohere), France (Mistral), and Israel (AI21). Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Do Models Exhibit Systematic Ordering Bias When Selecting Among Options? (RQ4) ‣ Experiments ‣ CCD-Bench: Probing Cultural Conflict in Large Language Model Decision-Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the KL-divergence heatmap and dendrogram.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Clustering Patterns.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dendrogram in Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Do Models Exhibit Systematic Ordering Bias When Selecting Among Options? (RQ4) ‣ Experiments ‣ CCD-Bench: Probing Cultural Conflict in Large Language Model Decision-Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reveals tight intra-family groupings at low divergence levels (e.g., Meta’s Llama-3.3 and Llama-4 at 0.0033, Anthropic’s Claude-3.7 and Claude-4 at 0.0398, Google’s Gemini-2.0 and Gemini-2.5 at 0.0103, OpenAI’s GPT-4o and GPT-4.1 at 0.0200, Microsoft’s Phi-4 and GPT-4.1 at 0.0032 via linkage). Broader clusters emerge around 0.01–0.05: one subgroup includes Google, OpenAI, xAI, Cohere, and Qwen (divergences <math id="Sx4.SSx5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.03 within); another features Anthropic, Meta, Perplexity, Mistral, and DeepSeek (similarly low internal divergences).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Correlation with Geographic Provenance.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Clustering shows weak geographic ties: US models span both subclusters, with corporate lineage (e.g., Meta/Anthropic vs. Google/OpenAI) dominating over location. Non-US models fragment similarly (Chinese: DeepSeek in subcluster 2, Qwen in 1; European-adjacent: Mistral in 2, Cohere in 1; AI21 with Google/Cohere). This implies that alignment strategies and shared corpora influence cultural behavior more than national origin, though Western-centric data limits distinctions. A notable limitation is our exclusive use of English prompts, which may bias outputs toward Anglo-American values and obscure regional nuances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong, Yun, and Sun 2024</a>)</cite>; future work should utilize multilingual prompts to distinguish between language and cultural effects.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce <span id="Sx5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CCD-Bench</span>, a novel benchmark specifically designed to assess how contemporary LLMs navigate conflicts among legitimate cultural values. Evaluating 17 LLMs on 2,182 dilemmas, we observed a pronounced cultural trend toward Nordic and Germanic clusters, hallmarks of egalitarian, consensus-oriented reasoning, while perspectives rooted in Eastern Europe and the Middle East &amp; North Africa remained markedly under‐represented. Despite high rates of multi-dimensional rationales, models relied on a narrow set of GLOBE dimensions, while rarely invoking Assertiveness or Gender Egalitarianism (<math id="Sx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>3% each), suggesting an aversion to confrontational or gender-focused reasoning and indicating limited true pluralism in reasoning. Ordering biases were negligible due to our Stratified Latin Square design, and model similarities clustered more by developer lineage than geographic provenance.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These results underscore the need for more nuanced approaches to cultural dynamics in LLMs if they are to operate credibly within an increasingly pluralistic world. Future research could extend CCD-Bench to multilingual prompts, incorporate dynamic user interactions, or explore mitigation strategies, such as targeted fine-tuning, to foster more diverse and adaptable decision-making.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

</section>
